<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uesugi Harunori</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1751">1751</a>–<a href="/years/1822">1822</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Uesugi Yōzan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Uesugi Harunori, also known as Uesugi Yōzan, was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yonezawa">Yonezawa</a> Domain in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> from 1767 to 1785. Facing near bankruptcy, he cut expenses, encouraged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sericulture">sericulture</a>, weaving, lacquer trees and carp ponds, reclaimed land and revived the domain school, the Kōjōkan. He continued to guide policy after retiring. He is celebrated as a model reforming lord, and his maxim on perseverance is widely quoted in Japan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uesugi-harunori.jpg" alt="Uesugi Harunori" width="320" height="346" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 不明。 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E4%B8%8A%E6%9D%89%E9%B7%B9%E5%B1%B1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
